sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because form differences are attained by growth at specific velocities over long periods, differences between any two related individuals or species must appear gradually and be little apparent in the young, and animals of very different adult size differ less and less in size and form when traced back through foetal stages.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Differences between related forms emerge gradually and are small in the young";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter III, “The Rate of Growth”; topic “Darwin and embryonic resemblance”. Scope: related individuals and species, especially mammals with foetal development"
}
